"""
Exp - Ulysse CSL Virginia oil spill Oct 2018 Sentinel-1 radar

On 7 Oct 2018 the ferry Ulysse hit the container ship CSL Virginia, anchored about 28 km north of Cap Corse, and fuel leaked into the sea. Sentinel-1 radar saw the slick clearly on 8 and 9 Oct and glimpsed possible drifting fragments on 13 Oct; aircraft did the daily tracking. From 14 Oct the oil had broken into tarballs and the wind had risen, and radar could no longer see it. Oil reached the beaches of the Var from 16 Oct; Cedre and Météo-France link it to this spill.

Three radar layers (open one at a time):
1. 8 Oct, evening: a dark slick about 35 km long leaves the ships (bright dot).
2. 9 Oct, evening: 24 hours later the slick is 50-60 km long. The large dark area south-east of the ships is calm water, not oil.
3. 13 Oct: dark fragments 60-80 km north-west of the ships, possibly part of the drifting oil (not confirmed). Dark areas near the coasts are sheltered from the wind; radar cannot tell whether oil is inside them.

How to read the radar: oil calms the small waves, so a slick is a dark patch with sharp edges; rough sea is light grey and ships are bright dots. Calm water and sea sheltered by land are also dark, so not every dark patch is oil. All layers share one grey scale, so a brighter sea means a stronger wind. Straight lines across the sea are instrument seams.

Method, sources and caveats: openeo-studio-scenarios, scenarios/ulysse-oil-spill-2018/README.md

Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data 2018 (RTC processing by Catalyst, CC BY 4.0), via Microsoft Planetary Computer. Land mask: GLOBE (NOAA NGDC).
"""


def one_pass(start, end):
    """All sentinel-1-rtc VV slices acquired between start and end (UTC), as one time step."""
    cube = DataCube(
        graph=PGNode(
            "load_collection",
            id="sentinel-1-rtc",
            spatial_extent=bounding_box,
            temporal_extent=[start, end],
            bands=["vv"],
        )
    )
    return cube.process("apply_pixel_selection", pixel_selection="first", data=cube)


def grey_ramp(lo, hi, step=0.5):
    """Grey in `step` dB classes, lo dB black to hi dB white; below/above are clipped to black/white."""
    n = round((hi - lo) / step)
    grey = [[[lo + i * step, lo + (i + 1) * step], [round(255 * (i + 0.5) / n)] * 3 + [255]] for i in range(n)]
    return [[[-80, lo], [0, 0, 0, 255]]] + grey + [[[hi, 40], [255, 255, 255, 255]]]


# One grey scale for every layer, so a brighter sea means a stronger wind: wind is the main clue that tells
# oil from look-alikes.
DB_COLORMAP = grey_ramp(-26, -10)

